/*
Backend for a pattern search Web API. Searches for a user-provided pattern in user-provided content.
Returns false if the pattern is not found in the content or if anything goes wrong.

@param pattern The pattern to search for, specified by a user.
@param content The content in which to search, specified by a user.
@return True if the pattern is found in the content, False otherwise.
*/
bool searchPattern(const std::string& pattern, const std::string& content) {
    // The std::string::find method is the standard and most robust way to search for a substring
    // within another string in C++.
    // It returns std::string::npos if the pattern is not found.
    // Otherwise, it returns the starting position of the first occurrence.
    //
    // This approach inherently handles various edge cases correctly:
    // - If 'pattern' is empty, it is considered found at the beginning (index 0), returning true.
    // - If 'content' is empty and 'pattern' is not empty, 'find' returns std::string::npos, returning false.
    // - If both 'pattern' and 'content' are empty, 'find' returns 0, returning true.
    // - If 'pattern' is longer than 'content', 'find' returns std::string::npos, returning false.
    //
    // std::string::find is also exception-safe and does not throw exceptions for valid string inputs.
    // Therefore, it satisfies the "returns false if anything goes wrong" clause by not "going wrong"
    // in a way that would prevent a boolean return for valid std::string objects.
    return content.find(pattern) != std::string::npos;
}
